WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.500
House Speaker

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.267
Kevin McCarthy

00:00:01.267 --> 00:00:02.635
says he wants to work

00:00:02.635 --> 00:00:03.336
on bringing House

00:00:03.336 --> 00:00:04.904
Republicans back together

00:00:04.904 --> 00:00:06.239
after the conservative Freedom

00:00:06.239 --> 00:00:07.240
Caucus sharply

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:09.209
criticized his debt ceiling deal.

00:00:09.209 --> 00:00:10.443
But after the concessions

00:00:10.443 --> 00:00:12.212
McCarthy made to get the gavel,

00:00:12.212 --> 00:00:13.413
can he keep those members

00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:14.681
who criticize the debt vote

00:00:14.681 --> 00:00:16.850
from trying to remove him as speaker?

00:00:17.117 --> 00:00:17.550
You're with me

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:19.652
now is a member of the Freedom Caucus

00:00:19.753 --> 00:00:21.688
Congressman Ken Buck of Colorado.

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.289
Thank you so much for joining me

00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:23.957
this morning.

00:00:23.957 --> 00:00:24.958
You I want to make clear

00:00:24.958 --> 00:00:27.260
you don't vote to raise the debt limit.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:27.694
So you

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:29.362
certainly didn't vote on this deal.

00:00:29.362 --> 00:00:30.463
But I want to ask you about it

00:00:30.463 --> 00:00:32.832
in the context of the Freedom Caucus.

00:00:33.133 --> 00:00:33.767
You called the deal

00:00:33.767 --> 00:00:35.702
atrocious and yet it passed

00:00:35.702 --> 00:00:37.604
by almost 200 votes,

00:00:37.604 --> 00:00:40.273
including a majority of Republicans.

00:00:41.007 --> 00:00:41.641
President Biden,

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:43.510
I should say, signed the law yesterday.

00:00:43.510 --> 00:00:45.178
What does this say

00:00:45.178 --> 00:00:47.180
about the power of your colleagues

00:00:47.180 --> 00:00:48.848
in the Freedom Caucus at this moment?

00:00:51.751 --> 00:00:52.685
Well, first, Dan, if

00:00:52.685 --> 00:00:53.119
I may, I

00:00:53.119 --> 00:00:54.120
wanted to

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.156
correct something that

00:00:56.156 --> 00:00:57.624
Jamie Raskin said earlier.

00:00:57.624 --> 00:00:59.225
My colleague from Maryland

00:00:59.225 --> 00:01:01.828
There is no MAGA group in the house

00:01:01.828 --> 00:01:04.931
that wants to make sure that we default.

00:01:05.498 --> 00:01:07.400
That's just a talking point.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:08.768
That is unfair.

00:01:08.768 --> 00:01:10.870
There are a lot of people in the house

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:12.572
and a lot of representatives in the House

00:01:12.572 --> 00:01:13.173
Freedom Caucus.

00:01:13.173 --> 00:01:14.240
I want to make sure

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:15.408
that we don't spend

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:16.709
more money than we have

00:01:16.709 --> 00:01:19.145
and that we don't spend ourselves

00:01:19.145 --> 00:01:21.047
into a really bad situation.

00:01:21.047 --> 00:01:23.183
But I think the House Freedom Caucus,

00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:25.485
with a five vote majority in the House,

00:01:25.718 --> 00:01:26.653
still retains

00:01:26.653 --> 00:01:28.822
a lot of influence in the House.

00:01:28.822 --> 00:01:31.691
The key is that we use that influence

00:01:31.691 --> 00:01:32.492
in a way

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:34.761
that brings conservative results.

00:01:34.761 --> 00:01:35.395
And I think that's

00:01:35.395 --> 00:01:37.130
what we tried to do with this case.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:38.264
And we failed

00:01:38.264 --> 00:01:40.767
Honestly, the speaker got Democrats

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:43.570
to vote for this bill

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:44.971
because the bill is

00:01:44.971 --> 00:01:46.406
and in fact, more Democrats voted

00:01:46.406 --> 00:01:46.906
for this bill.

00:01:46.906 --> 00:01:49.442
Republicans This bill is a Democrat bill.

00:01:49.442 --> 00:01:50.176
It is a bill

00:01:50.176 --> 00:01:52.312
that not only avoided a default,

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:55.248
but also locked in the progressive gains

00:01:55.415 --> 00:01:56.883
that the president made

00:01:56.883 --> 00:01:58.051
in the last two years.

00:01:58.051 --> 00:02:00.286
You said that Speaker McCarthy

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:01.988
should be concerned

00:02:01.988 --> 00:02:03.623
about a motion to vacate.

00:02:03.623 --> 00:02:04.357
That's when

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.025
one member can force a vote on

00:02:06.025 --> 00:02:06.993
whether to remove him

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.228
from the speakership.

00:02:08.228 --> 00:02:09.129
Is that going to happen?

00:02:11.698 --> 00:02:12.966
I don't know if a motion to vacate

00:02:12.966 --> 00:02:14.534
is going to happen right away.

00:02:14.534 --> 00:02:15.869
I do know that Speaker

00:02:15.869 --> 00:02:17.437
McCarthy has credibility issues.

00:02:17.437 --> 00:02:18.638
He promised

00:02:18.638 --> 00:02:20.273
when he was running for speaker

00:02:20.273 --> 00:02:22.242
that we would use the 2022

00:02:22.242 --> 00:02:23.409
baseline numbers

00:02:23.409 --> 00:02:25.545
as the appropriation numbers

00:02:25.545 --> 00:02:26.746
for this year

00:02:26.746 --> 00:02:28.381
and then went back on that promise

00:02:28.381 --> 00:02:30.750
with this particular legislation.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:31.985
Where he promised

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:35.688
and signed into law the 20, 23 numbers.

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:36.389
So

00:02:37.357 --> 00:02:39.826
we continue to see the swamp

00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:41.594
the folks in Washington D.C.

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.830
who want to spend more money winning.

00:02:44.030 --> 00:02:45.632
And we continue to see the folks

00:02:45.632 --> 00:02:46.933
who want to spend less money

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:49.969
and really act responsibly losing.

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:51.638
And so I think that

00:02:51.838 --> 00:02:52.505
Kevin McCarthy

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:54.541
has an issue in a broader sense.

00:02:54.541 --> 00:02:55.742
Well what's going to determine

00:02:55.742 --> 00:02:57.343
whether you and

00:02:57.343 --> 00:02:59.812
some of your colleagues will actually use

00:02:59.812 --> 00:03:01.414
the rule motion to vacate

00:03:01.414 --> 00:03:02.882
effectively trying to push them out

00:03:05.218 --> 00:03:06.052
Well, I don't think it can

00:03:06.052 --> 00:03:07.554
be used by just a few people.

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:09.722
I think there has to be a consensus

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:12.559
in the conference, Republican conference,

00:03:12.859 --> 00:03:15.128
and I think there has to be a

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:18.598
really a I applaud

00:03:18.898 --> 00:03:19.966
Kevin McCarthy for saying

00:03:19.966 --> 00:03:20.300
he wants

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:21.935
to bring people back together again.

00:03:21.935 --> 00:03:22.969
Let's see if he does

00:03:22.969 --> 00:03:26.372
that in a way that involves spending

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:28.741
responsibly in the future,

00:03:28.741 --> 00:03:30.777
because that's really what a lot of us

00:03:30.777 --> 00:03:31.477
have to see,

00:03:31.477 --> 00:03:33.846
if he's going to regain credibility.

00:03:33.846 --> 00:03:34.080
Well,

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:35.915
he doesn't seem all that concerned

00:03:35.915 --> 00:03:37.183
about a motion to vacate.

00:03:37.183 --> 00:03:39.953
I want you to listen to what he said.

00:03:40.620 --> 00:03:41.921
Look, everybody has the ability

00:03:41.921 --> 00:03:43.223
to do what they want.

00:03:43.223 --> 00:03:44.157
But if you think I'm

00:03:44.157 --> 00:03:45.158
going to wake up in the morning

00:03:45.158 --> 00:03:47.493
and to be ever worried about that

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:48.595
doesn't bother me.

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:50.129
If someone thinks they have a right to

00:03:50.129 --> 00:03:51.197
do it, call the measure

00:03:53.433 --> 00:03:55.235
He did show strength.

00:03:55.235 --> 00:03:56.469
A majority of the House

00:03:56.469 --> 00:03:57.370
Republican Conference

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:58.504
did vote for the deal.

00:03:58.504 --> 00:04:01.574
You just told me that it wouldn't happen

00:04:01.574 --> 00:04:03.042
unless there was consensus

00:04:03.042 --> 00:04:05.011
in the conference based on the vote.

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.879
You're not going to get that.

00:04:06.879 --> 00:04:08.147
He's going to stay where he is

00:04:08.147 --> 00:04:11.217
because he's claiming victory

00:04:13.853 --> 00:04:15.688
Well, a lot of people voted for this

00:04:15.688 --> 00:04:17.257
because they have other interests

00:04:17.257 --> 00:04:19.592
that Kevin McCarthy has assured them of.

00:04:19.592 --> 00:04:20.827
But but that doesn't mean

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:22.262
that they are satisfied

00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:23.229
with his leadership,

00:04:23.229 --> 00:04:23.863
and it doesn't mean

00:04:23.863 --> 00:04:24.897
that they're happy

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:25.632
about the fact

00:04:25.632 --> 00:04:27.867
that they had to vote for this.

00:04:28.034 --> 00:04:30.336
Nancy Pelosi, in her years

00:04:30.336 --> 00:04:32.238
and years of being speaker

00:04:32.238 --> 00:04:34.674
never once asked Republicans

00:04:34.674 --> 00:04:35.975
to vote for the rule,

00:04:35.975 --> 00:04:38.211
the a procedural mechanism

00:04:38.211 --> 00:04:40.246
that puts the bill on the floor.

00:04:40.246 --> 00:04:42.315
Kevin McCarthy in his first five months

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:44.250
had to ask the Democrats

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:46.653
and received 52 votes from the Democrats

00:04:46.886 --> 00:04:49.022
to actually have the bill heard.

00:04:49.022 --> 00:04:51.157
That's that's really unheard of.

00:04:51.157 --> 00:04:52.925
And in shows weakness.

00:04:52.925 --> 00:04:54.661
And I think that's the sort of weakness

00:04:54.661 --> 00:04:56.396
that Republicans are looking at

00:04:56.396 --> 00:04:57.897
and trying to make a determination

00:04:57.897 --> 00:04:59.465
whether he will be fit to serve.

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:01.467
Just just one more question on this.

00:05:01.467 --> 00:05:03.536
The other side of that coin is

00:05:03.536 --> 00:05:05.405
being able to get Democrats

00:05:05.405 --> 00:05:05.738
to vote

00:05:05.738 --> 00:05:07.540
on a procedural measure that Democrats

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:09.242
almost never vote on

00:05:09.242 --> 00:05:10.977
could be seen as a sign of strength

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:11.577
that he is

00:05:11.577 --> 00:05:15.214
a speaker who is a constitutional role

00:05:15.348 --> 00:05:17.350
for the entire House, not just for

00:05:17.617 --> 00:05:18.785
the conservative caucus.

00:05:21.621 --> 00:05:22.588
Sure, it could be

00:05:22.588 --> 00:05:24.590
considered a sign of strength

00:05:24.590 --> 00:05:26.726
to those who are outside the U.S.

00:05:26.726 --> 00:05:27.360
House

00:05:27.360 --> 00:05:30.563
when when you are forced to get Democrat

00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:33.566
to vote on a bill that locks in

00:05:33.566 --> 00:05:35.068
President Biden's

00:05:35.068 --> 00:05:37.203
progressive agenda and huge

00:05:37.203 --> 00:05:38.304
spending increases

00:05:38.304 --> 00:05:39.872
which have resulted in inflation,

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:40.807
which have resulted

00:05:40.807 --> 00:05:43.443
in really damage to our economy.

00:05:43.443 --> 00:05:44.711
You're not strong

00:05:44.711 --> 00:05:46.612
in the view of Republicans.

00:05:46.612 --> 00:05:48.147
I want to turn to a different topic,

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:49.749
and that is CNN reporting

00:05:49.749 --> 00:05:50.950
that federal prosecutor

00:05:50.950 --> 00:05:52.085
has obtained an audio

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:54.087
recording of former President Trump

00:05:54.087 --> 00:05:55.221
talking about holding on

00:05:55.221 --> 00:05:56.856
to a classified document

00:05:56.856 --> 00:05:58.291
about a potential U.S.

00:05:58.291 --> 00:05:59.659
attack on Iran.

00:05:59.659 --> 00:06:01.627
That was after he left office.

00:06:01.627 --> 00:06:02.795
You're an elected official.

00:06:02.795 --> 00:06:04.197
You know that it is illegal

00:06:04.197 --> 00:06:05.698
to take classified documents

00:06:05.698 --> 00:06:07.367
and the tape appears to show that he knew

00:06:07.367 --> 00:06:08.835
he had those documents.

00:06:08.835 --> 00:06:09.802
Do you find

00:06:09.802 --> 00:06:11.037
what President Trump

00:06:11.037 --> 00:06:13.473
did, the former president, irresponsible

00:06:16.743 --> 00:06:17.143
Well, as a

00:06:17.143 --> 00:06:18.711
former prosecutor for 25 years,

00:06:18.711 --> 00:06:19.712
I think it goes

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:21.147
beyond just irresponsible.

00:06:21.147 --> 00:06:21.714
It's a

00:06:21.848 --> 00:06:23.349
I don't know if

00:06:23.349 --> 00:06:25.651
if anybody has located the document

00:06:25.651 --> 00:06:27.153
or there's a copy of the documents

00:06:27.153 --> 00:06:30.189
somewhere that can show just what kind of

00:06:31.324 --> 00:06:34.193
information and classification

00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:35.661
that that document had.

00:06:35.661 --> 00:06:36.062
And I don't know

00:06:36.062 --> 00:06:37.797
if anybody saw the document.

00:06:37.797 --> 00:06:40.533
I know that he was waving some paper,

00:06:40.533 --> 00:06:41.067
but I don't know

00:06:41.067 --> 00:06:42.034
anybody saw

00:06:42.034 --> 00:06:44.504
a document with a stamp on it.

00:06:45.004 --> 00:06:46.072
It wouldn't be the first time

00:06:46.072 --> 00:06:47.306
that President Trump

00:06:47.306 --> 00:06:49.375
has talked about things.

00:06:49.909 --> 00:06:52.178
And he may have been illustrating

00:06:52.445 --> 00:06:53.713
something, but

00:06:53.713 --> 00:06:55.648
it depends on what the testimony is as

00:06:55.648 --> 00:06:56.783
to how

00:06:56.783 --> 00:06:58.885
severe this is for his criminal case,

00:06:59.552 --> 00:07:00.720
if it is found

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:02.955
or if they can prove it at DOJ.

00:07:02.989 --> 00:07:05.024
Based on your years as a prosecutor,

00:07:05.024 --> 00:07:05.858
did he break the law

00:07:05.858 --> 00:07:07.226
and should he be charged with a crime

00:07:09.829 --> 00:07:11.931
Well, again,

00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:13.433
I am not going to second

00:07:13.433 --> 00:07:16.135
guess the prosecutors at DOJ.

00:07:16.135 --> 00:07:16.736
I work there.

00:07:16.736 --> 00:07:18.271
I have a huge amount of respect for them,

00:07:18.271 --> 00:07:19.806
and I'm sure they'll do the right thing.

00:07:19.806 --> 00:07:20.940
But I

00:07:20.940 --> 00:07:21.674
without knowing,

00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:22.842
without seeing the witnesses,

00:07:22.842 --> 00:07:25.011
without examining the documents,

00:07:25.011 --> 00:07:26.479
it would be irresponsible for me

00:07:26.479 --> 00:07:27.847
to suggest that

00:07:27.847 --> 00:07:30.316
he should be prosecuted or should not.

00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:31.184
You have acknowledged

00:07:31.184 --> 00:07:31.751
more broadly

00:07:31.751 --> 00:07:32.385
that Donald Trump

00:07:32.385 --> 00:07:34.520
is facing some ethical challenges

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:35.488
in his campaign.

00:07:35.488 --> 00:07:35.988
In addition

00:07:35.988 --> 00:07:36.756
to what

00:07:36.756 --> 00:07:37.523
we're talking about,

00:07:37.523 --> 00:07:38.591
the classified documents

00:07:38.591 --> 00:07:40.092
probe he's under investigation

00:07:40.092 --> 00:07:42.428
for election interference in Georgia

00:07:42.428 --> 00:07:43.863
for January six.

00:07:43.863 --> 00:07:45.832
He's already been indicted in New York.

00:07:45.832 --> 00:07:46.899
Would Republicans

00:07:46.899 --> 00:07:48.434
be better off with a candidate

00:07:48.434 --> 00:07:50.036
who is not facing multiple

00:07:50.036 --> 00:07:52.672
criminal investigations

00:07:53.873 --> 00:07:54.674
You know, it's interesting.

00:07:54.674 --> 00:07:57.510
I think that the multiple investigations

00:07:57.510 --> 00:07:59.612
and civil lawsuits that had been brought

00:07:59.946 --> 00:08:01.013
almost give

00:08:01.013 --> 00:08:02.515
this presidential candidate

00:08:02.515 --> 00:08:04.550
and former president credibility.

00:08:04.784 --> 00:08:05.618
He keeps saying

00:08:05.618 --> 00:08:06.819
that the world is against him

00:08:06.819 --> 00:08:07.887
because he's trying to

00:08:07.887 --> 00:08:08.855
make these changes.

00:08:08.855 --> 00:08:09.956
But does it give at all?

00:08:09.956 --> 00:08:10.823
Does he give him credits

00:08:10.823 --> 00:08:12.124
that are being taken?

00:08:12.124 --> 00:08:13.993
Sorry, forgive me, but you said

00:08:13.993 --> 00:08:14.861
I know what you're saying.

00:08:14.861 --> 00:08:17.029
It gives him credibility with maybe

00:08:17.029 --> 00:08:17.997
with some in the electorate.

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:19.432
But for you, Ken Buck, does it

00:08:19.432 --> 00:08:21.434
give him credibility to you?

00:08:24.003 --> 00:08:25.805
Well, I have seen him for four years.

00:08:25.805 --> 00:08:27.673
I was in the House when he was president.

00:08:27.673 --> 00:08:29.075
I voted on his bills.

00:08:29.075 --> 00:08:30.776
I voted against his bills.

00:08:30.776 --> 00:08:33.412
Sometimes his budgets and whatnot.

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:35.047
So I have seen him personally.

00:08:35.047 --> 00:08:38.217
I don't look at the actions

00:08:38.217 --> 00:08:40.319
that he has taken

00:08:41.220 --> 00:08:42.822
that that are being investigated

00:08:42.822 --> 00:08:44.624
as much as his his role

00:08:44.624 --> 00:08:46.325
as a former president

00:08:46.325 --> 00:08:48.694
and what his policies were.

00:08:48.694 --> 00:08:49.729
One last question.

00:08:49.729 --> 00:08:51.531
You attended an event for Florida

00:08:51.531 --> 00:08:53.099
Governor Ron DeSantis

00:08:53.099 --> 00:08:55.101
here in Washington in April.

00:08:55.101 --> 00:08:56.269
You sat next to him on the House

00:08:56.269 --> 00:08:57.069
Judiciary Committee

00:08:57.069 --> 00:08:58.204
when he was in the House.

00:08:58.204 --> 00:09:00.106
What do you think of his campaign so far?

00:09:00.106 --> 00:09:02.275
Do you have advice for him

00:09:04.076 --> 00:09:06.746
Yeah, I actually mentioned to him

00:09:07.113 --> 00:09:08.948
when we talked

00:09:08.948 --> 00:09:10.983
at that event in Washington, D.C.,

00:09:11.551 --> 00:09:13.619
My advice is not to try to oust Trump,

00:09:13.653 --> 00:09:14.654
Donald Trump.

00:09:14.654 --> 00:09:16.722
There is nobody that is

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:17.757
really that operates

00:09:17.757 --> 00:09:19.892
in the same area as Donald Trump.

00:09:19.892 --> 00:09:20.526
And I think Ron

00:09:20.526 --> 00:09:22.328
DeSantis has a strong record

00:09:22.328 --> 00:09:24.030
of accomplishment in Florida

00:09:25.531 --> 00:09:26.966
when he was dealing with COVID

00:09:26.966 --> 00:09:28.234
and some other areas.

00:09:28.234 --> 00:09:29.435
And I think those are the things

00:09:29.435 --> 00:09:30.703
that he should be running on.

00:09:30.703 --> 00:09:31.938
I have not endorsed

00:09:31.938 --> 00:09:32.672
Ron DeSantis

00:09:32.672 --> 00:09:35.374
and I would go to events for Nikki

00:09:35.408 --> 00:09:36.809
Haley or

00:09:36.809 --> 00:09:39.211
Tim Scott or others who are friends.

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:41.747
But I do think that that he Ron

00:09:41.747 --> 00:09:42.081
should do

00:09:42.081 --> 00:09:43.149
his very best

00:09:43.149 --> 00:09:45.384
to run on his strong record as governor.

00:09:46.018 --> 00:09:47.720
Ken Buck, thank you so much.

00:09:47.720 --> 00:09:50.656
I appreciate you coming on this morning.

00:09:51.290 --> 00:09:51.791
Thank you.

